messages:
acme-support: Hey team — customer acme-pay reported a midday surge (12:03 PT) where their client got a cascade of 429s. Their SDK retried immediately with no jitter and they ended up with duplicate payment attempts. Can we triage?

maya: Can you paste a sample request id + response headers? Also which SDK version are they on?

acme-support: sample headers attached in their transcript: 
HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
Retry-After: 2
x-rw-quota-remaining: 0
x-rw-quota-reset: 1670000596
Request-Id: req_8f3c2b
Client: acme-sdk-js v1.2.0

devon: I pulled our logs for req_8f3c2b. We definitely returned Retry-After: 2 and quota headers. Looks like the client ignored Retry-After and retried N concurrent requests in ~200ms intervals. That aligns with their SDK defaults (v1.2.0 uses linear backoff 500ms w/ no jitter).

sofia: Confirming — v1.2.0 default retry: attempts=3, delay=500ms linear, no jitter. v1.3.0 switched to exponential backoff with full jitter and respects Retry-After when present.

sofia: For customers who can't upgrade immediately, recommend they implement this pattern (full jitter + honor Retry-After):
```js
// pseudo-code: full jitter exponential backoff
async function retry(fn, attempts=5) {
  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      if (!isRetryable(err)) throw err;
      const base = Math.min(1000 * Math.pow(2, i), 30000); // cap 30s
      const wait = Math.random() * base; // full jitter
      if (err.headers && err.headers['retry-after']) {
        await sleep(parseInt(err.headers['retry-after'], 10) * 1000);
      } else {
        await sleep(wait);
      }
    }
  }
  throw new Error('retries exhausted');
}
```

sofia: Also strongly advise including an Idempotency-Key header for payment endpoints so duplicate retries are safely deduped server-side. Example header: `Idempotency-Key: <uuid-v4>`

devon: Additional guidance: treat 429s as signals to slow down global concurrency. Client-side token-bucket or leaky-bucket pacing (limit concurrent inflight requests per customer to N) prevents bursts from overwhelming shared quota.

sofia: Repro steps to validate on our side: we can simulate a client with 100 concurrent workers sending POST /v1/charge with no idempotency key and default SDK retry to reproduce the burst and 429 cascade. Example stress curl loop: 
```bash
for i in {1..200}; do curl -s -X POST https://api.redwood.ai/v1/charge -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -H "Idempotency-Key:" -d '{"amt":100}' &
done
wait
```
(note: omitting idempotency intentionally to reproduce dupes)

acme-support: Customer asked if we can auto-dedup charges on our side if they didn't send Idempotency-Key.

aamir: Billing note — we can look up potentially duplicate charges by matching request metadata (customer id, timestamp window, amount) but it's not 100% reliable without idempotency keys. I'll put a temporary invoice hold for the flagged charges and follow up with the customer. Please send list of request-ids.

maya: Action items: 1) Ask customer to upgrade to SDK v1.3.0 or implement full-jitter backoff + honor Retry-After. 2) Strongly require Idempotency-Key for payment endpoints (we'll mark in docs). 3) Billing (aamir) to triage suspected duplicates. 4) Engineering (devon) to evaluate if we should add stronger quota headers or a global soft-throttle response for spikes.

acme-support: Thanks all — I'll relay upgrade + idempotency guidance and collect request-ids for @aamir. Closing loop once customer confirms. :thumbsup:
